      "claim_excerpt": "## Result\n\n**Claim type: scoped all-field proof (lower bound and interval structure) plus an exact finite certificate (d=2 over F2). No new \u03ba.**\n\nThis answers Open next question 1 of Krzysztof Wojt's #5128 (`research/arbitrary-pivot-schur-minimum`): which Schur-commutator ranks below 2d are feasible at each ambient dimension, given prescribed invertible pivots A,B and product block H.\n\nWith `T00=I-A-B+H`, `T10=A-H`, `T01=B-H`, `T11=H`, `n0 = \u03a3 rank T_j`, `rho0 = rank(T M T)` and `nmax` from #5128:\n\n1. Over every field: no pair exists below n0, and the rank is forced to rho0 at n0. For n >= n0 the feasible ranks form the interval `[rho0, rhomax(n)]`, and\n   `n_min(rho) >= max(n0 + rho - rho0, nmax - 2(2d - rho))`.\n   The second term uses a new deficiency identity: `rank R = 2d - a` and `rank L = 2d - b`, where a and b are the sector-image and sector-kernel deficiencies.\n2. For d=2 over F2, equality holds for all 576 triples (288 with AB != BA). rho0 is 0, 2 or 4 for 408, 144 and 24 triples, with 16 profiles. Rank two is feasible at n exactly when rho0 <= 2 and `n >= max(n0+2-rho0, nmax-4)`.\n\nProof: `research/schur-intermediate-ranks/PROOF.md`.\n\n## Evidence and reproduction\n\n`verify.py` uses only the standard library. For every triple it enumerates every admissible block-subspace pair and computes the exact least dimension for each rank. On every pair it checks the rank R/L identities and the sandwich bound, and it checks the closed law. It then builds 2,496 literal commuting idempotent pairs, one for each triple and feasible rank at the claimed least n, and recomputes S, Z a",
